Cold, adversarial QA verification of COD-123 against the ticket below and this slice's diff against the base branch. Treat the code on disk and the ticket as the only sources of truth; your job is to find what does NOT work. Run only the tests relevant to this slice (the new or changed test files for this ticket) using the project's test runner (in a multi-workspace repo, the affected workspace's own runner) — not the whole suite. No separate QA brief was written for this tiny slice: derive the concrete, checkable behaviors yourself from the ticket and the diff, then confirm each actually holds.  Distinguish defects in this slice's own code from pre-existing or out-of-scope issues. Alongside correctness, read this slice's diff for classic code smells and name any you actually find, pointing at the concrete code: mysterious name, duplicated code, feature envy, data clumps, primitive obsession, repeated switches, divergent change, speculative generality, message chains, middleman. These are quality findings: record them in the verdict summary, and when the verdict already fails on correctness list them after the real failures so the repair pass can act on them. A smell never fails an otherwise-correct slice on its own — pass/fail stays governed by the acceptance criteria, the rubric, and the relevant tests. When finished, write a JSON verdict to exactly /tmp/verify-COD-123.json: {"pass": true|false, "summary": "one line", "failures": ["..."], "criteria": [{"text": "<the acceptance criterion>", "status": "satisfied"|"violated"|"unverified", "note": "what you observed, or why it could not be settled"}], "browser": "driven"|"skipped"|"not-applicable", "browser_notes": "what you exercised, or the concrete reason the browser could not be driven"}. Set pass=false if any relevant test fails or any behavior the ticket requires does not work; failures lists each concrete problem (empty when pass is true). Grade EVERY acceptance criterion in "criteria" — one entry per criterion, in the order the rubric lists them, or one per behavior you derived when no rubric was written. "satisfied" asserts an observation you actually made; when the decisive observation was skipped or unavailable — browser QA undriven, a tool or harness missing, an environment unreachable — the status is "unverified" and the note says what you could not observe. Inferring that a criterion holds because the code looks plausible is prohibited, and a criterion you observed to fail is "violated", which fails the whole verdict. Account for browser QA honestly: set "browser" to "driven" when you exercised this slice's UI in a real browser, "not-applicable" only for a backend-only slice with no UI surface, or "skipped" when a UI surface went undriven. This is an unattended run against a sanctioned, dedicated automation browser — never skip a UI surface out of concern for a user's session, and if the browser cannot connect, set "skipped" and report the concrete reason in "browser_notes" instead of silently omitting it. Do not commit, push, or open a PR.